::PC_U04_L02_Q01::
Question ID: PC_U04_L02_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.2
Question:
Which information is needed for A=1/2ab sin(C)?
{
~A. Three angles only#This choice does not match the formula structure required by the lesson.
=B. Two sides and the included angle#Correct. The formula uses two sides and the angle between them.
~C. One side only#This reflects a common setup error; identify the given sides and angles first.
~D. Perimeter only#This does not connect the triangle information to the correct trigonometric relationship.
}
::PC_U04_L02_Q02::
Question ID: PC_U04_L02_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.2
Question:
What is the included angle?
{
~A. Any outside angle#This choice does not match the formula structure required by the lesson.
~B. The smallest angle always#This reflects a common setup error; identify the given sides and angles first.
~C. An angle not between the given sides#This does not connect the triangle information to the correct trigonometric relationship.
=D. The angle between the two given sides#Correct. The included angle is formed by the two known sides.
}
::PC_U04_L02_Q03::
Question ID: PC_U04_L02_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.2
Question:
What is sin(30 degrees)?
{
=A. 1/2#Correct. sin(30 degrees)=1/2.
~B. 1#This choice does not match the formula structure required by the lesson.
~C. 0#This reflects a common setup error; identify the given sides and angles first.
~D. 2#This does not connect the triangle information to the correct trigonometric relationship.
}
::PC_U04_L02_Q04::
Question ID: PC_U04_L02_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.2
Question:
For sides 8 and 10 with included angle 60 degrees, which setup is correct?
{
~A. A=8+10+60#This choice does not match the formula structure required by the lesson.
~B. A=1/2(8)(60)sin(10)#This reflects a common setup error; identify the given sides and angles first.
=C. A=1/2(8)(10)sin(60)#Correct. Use one-half times the two sides times sine of the included angle.
~D. A=(8)(10)(60)#This does not connect the triangle information to the correct trigonometric relationship.
}
::PC_U04_L02_Q05::
Question ID: PC_U04_L02_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.2
Question:
Why does the sine factor appear in the area formula?
{
~A. It finds the perimeter#This choice does not match the formula structure required by the lesson.
~B. It changes area into volume#This reflects a common setup error; identify the given sides and angles first.
=C. It accounts for the height created by the angle#Correct. The sine factor gives the height relationship from the included angle.
~D. It removes the need for side lengths#This does not connect the triangle information to the correct trigonometric relationship.
}
